\f1\b \cf2 1. Find the missing term in the given series:
\f0\b0 \
\

\f1\b 4, 9, 19, 39, 79, ___?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) 119\
\
(B) 139\
\
(C) 159\
\
(D) 169\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) 159.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  The pattern is $(n \\times 2) + 1$.\
\
$(4 \\times 2) + 1 = 9$;\
\
$(9 \\times 2) + 1 = 19$;\
\
$(19 \\times 2) + 1 = 39$;\
\
$(39 \\times 2) + 1 = 79$;\
\
$(79 \\times 2) + 1 = 159$.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 2. In a certain code language, if 'PLANT' is coded as 'QMBOU', how will 'FLOWER' be coded in that same language?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) GMPXFS\
\
(B) GMPXES\
\
(C) GMQWFS\
\
(D) HNPYFT\
\

\f1\b Answer: (A) GMPXFS.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Each letter is shifted forward by $+1$ in alphabetical order ($\\text\{P\}\\to\\text\{Q\}, \\text\{L\}\\to\\text\{M\}, \\text\{A\}\\to\\text\{B\}$, etc.). For FLOWER: $\\text\{F\}+1=\\text\{G\}, \\text\{L\}+1=\\text\{M\}, \\text\{O\}+1=\\text\{P\}, \\text\{W\}+1=\\text\{X\}, \\text\{E\}+1=\\text\{F\}, \\text\{R\}+1=\\text\{S\}$.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 3. Choose the odd one out from the following group:
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Copper\
\
(B) Aluminium\
\
(C) Silver\
\
(D) Silicon\
\

\f1\b Answer: (D) Silicon.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Copper, aluminium, and silver are typical metallic conductors; silicon is a metalloid/semiconductor.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 4. A boy walks 12 meters North, turns right and walks 5 meters. How far is he from his starting point?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) 17 meters\
\
(B) 13 meters\
\
(C) 7 meters\
\
(D) 15 meters\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) 13 meters.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  By the Pythagorean theorem: $\\text\{Distance\} = \\sqrt\{12^2 + 5^2\} = \\sqrt\{144 + 25\} = \\sqrt\{169\} = 13\\text\{ m\}$.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 5. Pointing to a photograph of a woman, Rahul said, "She is the only daughter of my father's mother." How is the woman related to Rahul?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Mother\
\
(B) Sister\
\
(C) Paternal Aunt (Bua)\
\
(D) Grandmother\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) Paternal Aunt (Bua).
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Rahul's father's mother is his paternal grandmother. The only daughter of his grandmother is his father's sister (Rahul's paternal aunt).\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 6. Which of the following Venn diagrams best represents the relationship between: Animals, Herbivores, and Tigers?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Three mutually disjoint circles\
\
(B) A large circle containing two separate disjoint inner circles\
\
(C) Two intersecting circles inside a third circle\
\
(D) A large circle enclosing an inner circle, which encloses another circle\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) A large circle containing two separate disjoint inner circles.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Both Herbivores and Tigers belong to the category "Animals", but Tigers are strictly carnivores and share no overlap with herbivores.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 7. In a row of 37 students facing North, Ananya is 14th from the left end. What is her rank from the right end?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) 23rd\
\
(B) 24th\
\
(C) 25th\
\
(D) 22nd\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) 24th.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  $\\text\{Right Rank\} = (\\text\{Total Students\} - \\text\{Left Rank\}) + 1 = (37 - 14) + 1 = 23 + 1 = 24\\text\{th\}$.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 8. If '$+$' means '$\\div$', '$-$' means '$\\times$', '$\\times$' means '$+$', and '$\\div$' means '$-$', then evaluate:
\f0\b0 \
\
$$20 - 4 + 8 \\times 6 \\div 3$$\
(A) 13\
\
(B) 17\
\
(C) 11\
\
(D) 19\
\

\f1\b Answer: (A) 13.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Replacing operators: $20 \\times 4 \\div 8 + 6 - 3$.\
\
Applying BODMAS: $20 \\times (4/8) + 6 - 3 = (20 \\times 0.5) + 6 - 3 = 10 + 6 - 3 = 13$.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 9. Which letter replaces the question mark in the sequence: B, E, I, N, T, ___?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Y\
\
(B) Z\
\
(C) A\
\
(D) X\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) A.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Alphabet positions: $\\text\{B\}(2) \\xrightarrow\{+3\} \\text\{E\}(5) \\xrightarrow\{+4\} \\text\{I\}(9) \\xrightarrow\{+5\} \\text\{N\}(14) \\xrightarrow\{+6\} \\text\{T\}(20) \\xrightarrow\{+7\} 27$. In a 26-letter cycle, the 27th letter wraps around to $\\text\{A\}$.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 10. What will be the correct mirror image of the word "SCIENCE" when the mirror is placed vertically to its right?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) \uc0\u398 \u390 \u1048 \u398 I\u390 S\
\
(B) SCIENCE\
\
(C) S\uc0\u390 I\u398 \u1048 \u390 E\
\
(D) \uc0\u398 \u390 NEICS\
\

\f1\b Answer: (A) \uc0\u398 \u390 \u1048 \u398 I\u390 S.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Lateral inversion reverses the order from right to left while flipping asymmetric letters horizontally.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\li4320\fi-4320\ri4320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 Section 2: Core Science (Questions 11\'9645)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i\b0\fs24 \cf2 1 Mark Each
\f0\i0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 11. Which chemical reagent produces a dark blue-black coloration when added to a food item containing starch?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Dilute Copper Sulphate solution\
\
(B) Dilute Iodine solution\
\
(C) Caustic soda solution\
\
(D) Benedict's reagent\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) Dilute Iodine solution.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Iodine interacts with the amylose coils in starch to form a characteristic charge-transfer polyiodide complex that appears blue-black.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 12. Swollen and bleeding gums along with slow wound healing are characteristic deficiency symptoms of which vitamin?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Vitamin A\
\
(B) Vitamin B1\
\
(C) Vitamin C\
\
(D) Vitamin D\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) Vitamin C.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Scurvy is caused by Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) deficiency, which is essential for collagen synthesis.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 13. Which combination of leaf venation and root type is correctly matched?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Parallel venation \'97 Taproot system\
\
(B) Reticulate venation \'97 Fibrous root system\
\
(C) Reticulate venation \'97 Taproot system\
\
(D) Parallel venation \'97 Stilt root system\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) Reticulate venation \'97 Taproot system.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Dicot plants (e.g., pea, mango) exhibit reticulate venation with taproots. Monocots (e.g., grass, wheat) have parallel venation with fibrous roots.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 14. Which part of a flower develops into a fruit after successful fertilization?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Ovule\
\
(B) Stigma\
\
(C) Ovary\
\
(D) Petal\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) Ovary.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Post-fertilization, the ovary ripens into a fruit, while the fertilized ovules develop into seeds.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 15. The loss of excess water as water vapor through the microscopic stomata of leaves is called:
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Respiration\
\
(B) Transpiration\
\
(C) Guttation\
\
(D) Evacuation\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) Transpiration.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Transpiration creates suction pull that helps transport water and dissolved minerals from roots to high foliage.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 16. Which type of joint allows movement in only one plane, similar to the opening and closing of a door?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Ball and socket joint\
\
(B) Pivotal joint\
\
(C) Hinge joint\
\
(D) Fixed joint\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) Hinge joint.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Hinge joints (found in knees and elbows) restrict movement along a single axis (flexion and extension).\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 17. Earthworms lack bones. Which biological structures allow them to anchor their segmented body to the soil during locomotion?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Flagella\
\
(B) Tentacles\
\
(C) Bristles (Setae)\
\
(D) Webbed feet\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) Bristles (Setae).
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Earthworms use longitudinal and circular muscles along with tiny hair-like projections called setae to grip the ground.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 18. Why do desert plants like cacti possess stems that are thick, green, and covered with a waxy layer?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) To store food and trap burrowing animals\
\
(B) To carry out photosynthesis while minimizing water loss by transpiration\
\
(C) To deter all microorganisms from attacking root hairs\
\
(D) To help the plant float during sudden flash floods\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) To carry out photosynthesis while minimizing water loss by transpiration.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Leaves are modified into spines to reduce transpiration surface area; the green fleshy stem handles photosynthesis and stores water.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 19. Which respiratory organs are used by dolphins and whales to breathe atmospheric air?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Gills\
\
(B) Moist skin\
\
(C) Blowholes connected to lungs\
\
(D) Spiracles\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) Blowholes connected to lungs.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Cetaceans are mammals; they lack gills and must surface periodically to breathe through blowholes at the top of their heads into lungs.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 20. Which organism is predominantly used in the vermicomposting process to convert organic household waste into nutrient-rich compost?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Roundworms\
\
(B) Redworms (
\f2\i Eisenia fetida
\f0\i0 )\
\
(C) Flatworms\
\
(D) Leeches\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) Redworms (
\f3\i Eisenia fetida
\f1\i0 ).
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Redworms possess a specialized muscular structure called a gizzard that grinds down decaying plant matter.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 21. An object is placed between a light source and a screen. Which type of object will cast a distinct, dark shadow?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Transparent object\
\
(B) Translucent object\
\
(C) Opaque object\
\
(D) Luminous object\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) Opaque object.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Opaque objects completely block light rays traveling in straight paths, creating an umbra (dark shadow) on the screen.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 22. What is the fundamental property of light that accounts for both shadow formation and the operation of a pinhole camera?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Dispersion of light into spectral colors\
\
(B) Rectilinear propagation of light (light travels in straight lines)\
\
(C) Total internal reflection\
\
(D) Polarization of electromagnetic waves\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) Rectilinear propagation of light (light travels in straight lines).
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Because light does not bend around macroscopic corners spontaneously, straight ray trajectories yield both sharp geometric shadows and inverted pinhole images.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 23. Which image property is observed when viewing yourself in a clean, vertical plane mirror?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Inverted and diminished\
\
(B) Laterally inverted, virtual, and same size as object\
\
(C) Real, inverted, and magnified\
\
(D) Virtual, erect, and magnified\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) Laterally inverted, virtual, and same size as object.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Plane mirrors produce upright virtual images with unit magnification ($m = +1$) where left and right appear swapped (lateral inversion).\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 24. The SI unit for measuring length is:
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Centimeter\
\
(B) Kilometer\
\
(C) Meter\
\
(D) Millimeter\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) Meter.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Under the International System of Units (SI), the meter (m) is the base unit of length.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 25. Which type of motion is executed by the blades of a working ceiling fan?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Periodic and rectilinear motion\
\
(B) Circular / Rotational motion\
\
(C) Non-periodic oscillatory motion\
\
(D) Pure rectilinear motion\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) Circular / Rotational motion.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Points on the blades travel along circular trajectories centered on the fixed central axis of the rotor.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 26. Which of the following motions is strictly periodic?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) A car taking a turn along a mountain road\
\
(B) The swinging bob of an undisturbed grandfather pendulum\
\
(C) A flying butterfly in a garden\
\
(D) An apple falling from a branch\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) The swinging bob of an undisturbed grandfather pendulum.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Periodic motion repeats its path over equal, precise intervals of time.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 27. In an electric cell, electrical energy is produced from stored:
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Thermal energy\
\
(B) Chemical energy\
\
(C) Mechanical energy\
\
(D) Magnetic energy\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) Chemical energy.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Electrochemical redox reactions between internal electrolytes and electrodes liberate electrons, producing a potential difference.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 28. Why are copper wires normally coated with polyvinyl chloride (PVC) or rubber?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) PVC is a good electrical conductor\
\
(B) PVC is an electrical insulator that prevents short circuits and electric shocks\
\
(C) PVC decreases the weight of copper metals\
\
(D) PVC chemically reacts with copper to generate electricity\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) PVC is an electrical insulator that prevents short circuits and electric shocks.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Insulating sheaths prevent direct conductive contact with stray charges or living tissue.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 29. Which piece of material will complete an electrical gap and cause an electric bulb in a simple circuit to glow?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) A glass rod\
\
(B) A wooden matchstick\
\
(C) An iron nail\
\
(D) A plastic ruler\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) An iron nail.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Iron is a metallic conductor that allows charge carriers (electrons) to bridge open terminals.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 30. Which of the following is a non-magnetic material?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Iron\
\
(B) Cobalt\
\
(C) Nickel\
\
(D) Brass\
\

\f1\b Answer: (D) Brass.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Brass is an alloy of copper and zinc; neither element exhibits ferromagnetism at room temperature.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 31. When a bar magnet is suspended freely by a thread from its center of gravity, it naturally aligns along the:
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) East-West direction\
\
(B) North-South direction\
\
(C) North-East direction\
\
(D) South-West direction\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) North-South direction.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Earth acts as a dipole magnet; the magnetic north pole of the bar magnet is attracted toward Earth's geomagnetic North (which is magnetically an south-seeking pole).\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 32. What occurs when the North pole of a permanent magnet is brought close to the North pole of another freely suspended magnet?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Strong magnetic attraction\
\
(B) Magnetic repulsion\
\
(C) Neutralization with zero net force\
\
(D) Generation of electric sparks\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) Magnetic repulsion.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Like magnetic poles repel each other, whereas unlike poles attract.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 33. Which method is most effective for separating wheat grains from dry stalks after harvesting?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Sieving\
\
(B) Threshing\
\
(C) Winnowing\
\
(D) Decantation\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) Threshing.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Threshing beats dried stalks against hard surfaces (or uses mechanical threshers) to detach the grain seeds.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 34. Winnowing relies on which physical difference between the components of a mixture?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Difference in particle color\
\
(B) Difference in boiling points\
\
(C) Difference in weight/density when acted on by blowing wind\
\
(D) Difference in magnetic susceptibility\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) Difference in weight/density when acted on by blowing wind.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Wind carries lighter husk flakes farther away while heavier grain seeds drop vertically.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 35. Which separation sequence correctly isolates clear drinking water from muddy river water?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Evaporation followed by Threshing\
\
(B) Sedimentation, Decantation, followed by Filtration\
\
(C) Magnetic separation followed by Sieving\
\
(D) Winnowing followed by Distillation\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) Sedimentation, Decantation, followed by Filtration.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Heavy suspended mud particles settle first (sedimentation), the clearer top layer is poured off (decantation), and fine particles are trapped with filter paper (filtration).\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 36. What is a solution called when no more solute can dissolve in it at a given constant temperature?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Unsaturated solution\
\
(B) Saturated solution\
\
(C) Supersaturated emulsion\
\
(D) Dilute solution\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) Saturated solution.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  At dynamic saturation equilibrium, the rate of dissolution equals the rate of precipitation.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 37. Which of the following is a reversible physical change?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Rusting of an iron gate\
\
(B) Burning of a beeswax candle wick\
\
(C) Melting of ice into liquid water\
\
(D) Baking a loaf of bread\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) Melting of ice into liquid water.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Phase change from solid ice to liquid water involves no change in chemical composition and can be reversed by cooling below 0\'b0C.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 38. Which gas constitutes approximately 78% of Earth's atmosphere by volume?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Oxygen\
\
(B) Carbon dioxide\
\
(C) Argon\
\
(D) Nitrogen\
\

\f1\b Answer: (D) Nitrogen.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Clean, dry atmospheric air is composed of ~78.08% Nitrogen, ~20.95% Oxygen, ~0.93% Argon, and ~0.04% Carbon dioxide.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 39. Which component of air is essential for respiration in living organisms and supports the combustion of fuels?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Nitrogen\
\
(B) Oxygen\
\
(C) Carbon dioxide\
\
(D) Helium\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) Oxygen.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Oxygen acts as the terminal electron acceptor in aerobic respiration and sustains exothermic oxidation (combustion).\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 40. Why do mountaineers carry oxygen cylinders with them when climbing high Himalayan peaks?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) High-altitude air is saturated with toxic ozone\
\
(B) Atmospheric air density and oxygen partial pressure drop significantly at high altitudes\
\
(C) High altitudes lack gravity to hold air in place\
\
(D) To protect themselves against wild animals\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) Atmospheric air density and oxygen partial pressure drop significantly at high altitudes.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  As altitude increases, atmospheric pressure drops, making oxygen molecules per breath insufficient for bodily needs (hypoxia).\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 41. The continuous circulation of water from Earth's surface into the atmosphere and back through evaporation, condensation, and precipitation is known as the:
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Nitrogen cycle\
\
(B) Carbon cycle\
\
(C) Water cycle (Hydrological cycle)\
\
(D) Rock cycle\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) Water cycle (Hydrological cycle).
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Solar energy drives surface evaporation and transpiration; vapor condenses into clouds and precipitates as rain, snow, or hail.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 42. Which material allows almost all incident light to pass through it, enabling objects on the other side to be seen clearly?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Frosted shower glass\
\
(B) Clear optical glass\
\
(C) Plywood sheet\
\
(D) Cardboard sheet\
\

\f1\b Answer: (B) Clear optical glass.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Transparent materials transmit light with minimal scattering, rendering sharp visibility.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 43. Which pair of items will both float when placed on the surface of calm water?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Dry cork and wooden pencil\
\
(B) Iron coin and rubber eraser\
\
(C) Glass marble and steel spoon\
\
(D) Aluminum foil ball and ceramic tile\
\

\f1\b Answer: (A) Dry cork and wooden pencil.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Both cork and most seasoned woods have average densities less than that of liquid water ($1.0\\text\{ g/cm\}^3$).\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 44. What happens to the solubility of common salt or sugar in water when the temperature of the water is increased?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) It decreases dramatically\
\
(B) It remains completely unchanged\
\
(C) It increases\
\
(D) The solution turns into an insoluble gas\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) It increases.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  For most solid solutes like sucrose and salt, dissolution is endothermic; raising temperature increases kinetic energy and solvent capacity.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 45. Which of the following is classified as a non-biodegradable waste material?
\f0\b0 \
\
(A) Banana peels\
\
(B) Discarded newspaper\
\
(C) Polyethylene plastic bag\
\
(D) Cotton fabric scrap\
\

\f1\b Answer: (C) Polyethylene plastic bag.
\f0\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Explanation:
\f0\i0  Synthetic polyethylene lacks chemical bonds accessible to microbial enzyme degradation, persisting for centuries.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\li4320\fi-4320\ri4320\partightenfactor0

